# Affordance — engine side

Objects enter or leave a scene **only** via `addObject` / `removeObject`
deltas, each carrying a **`cause`**. Nothing else may alter a scene's object
set — not a flag, not a gate, not the generator after layout.

Both deltas are scene-scoped: `{scene, object, cause}`. An object added in one
room is added in that room, and a template dealt twice does not empty a pool
you never entered.

## the design law behind it (LAWS.md #affordance)

**Tappability is constant for an object's lifetime on screen.** An object never
becomes un-tappable while it is still visibly there; gates live in *responses*,
not in whether a thing can be touched.

> "It's a door." is a complete answer.

The first tap is always free (GAME.md #input): it describes, selects and
surfaces actions. It never advances the world, never costs, never harms. That
is bedrock and it has no exceptions — risk lives in the explicit investigation
actions the free tap reveals.

The engine enforces the mechanism; the author owes the cause.
